<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>APPENDIX</h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Experimental Details</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All methods share the same frozen <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> base policy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> and a
Pixel-SAC agent that acts in the noise latent space. Our method (E-QRL) additionally
learns an elastic schedule, i.e., the injected noise <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> together with the number
of denoising steps and the action-chunk length, under a compute (NFE) budget and a
<math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\mathrm{std}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>std</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\mathrm{std}}</annotation></semantics></math>-based difficulty signal. The DSRL baseline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>
uses the same SAC backbone but keeps the denoising steps and action chunk fixed and
removes all elastic modules (range, NFE budget, and difficulty). We
report the hyperparameters for the real-robot experiments (Table <a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 6.1 Experimental Details ‣ 6 APPENDIX ‣ Elastic Queries Reinforcement Learning: Self-Aware Policy Execution for VLA Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>),
the LIBERO simulation tasks (Table <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 6.1 Experimental Details ‣ 6 APPENDIX ‣ Elastic Queries Reinforcement Learning: Self-Aware Policy Execution for VLA Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), and the ALOHA simulation task
(Table <a href="#S6.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 6.1 Experimental Details ‣ 6 APPENDIX ‣ Elastic Queries Reinforcement Learning: Self-Aware Policy Execution for VLA Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Hyperparameters whose meaning is shared across environments use the same row name in
all tables. “Training steps” denotes gradient steps for the offline setting and
environment-interaction steps for the online settings (both capped by <span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">max_steps</span>).
“Grad steps / collection” is the number of SAC updates performed after each data
collection round (<span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">train_steps_per_collection</span> for the online real-robot setting,
<span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">multi_grad_step</span> for simulation; the offline setting has no collection round).
For the DSRL baseline, “–” marks hyperparameters that are specific to E-QRL and have
no counterpart in the baseline.</p>
</div>
